  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:36px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="article">
        <p class="lede">Most remote hires fail for a boring reason: the process was borrowed from in-person hiring, where you can read a room and fix misunderstandings at the coffee machine. Remote hiring has to test in writing, on camera, and through real work. Here is the process, step by step, whether you run it yourself or have a partner run it for you.</p>

        <h2>Step 1: Define the role before you look at a single candidate</h2>
        <p>The biggest mistake in remote hiring is skipping the role specification. Write down, in one document:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The 5 to 8 recurring outcomes the role owns, stated as results, not activities</li>
          <li>The tools they will use daily</li>
          <li>The hours and time zone overlap required</li>
          <li>The 30, 60, and 90 day success measures</li>
          <li>Who they report to, and how often they check in</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you cannot write the daily tasks down in 30 minutes, the role is not ready to hire for, in any country. Vague roles attract mismatched candidates and produce unfocused interviews. If you are not sure the workload justifies a full-time hire yet, check the <a href="../../blog/signs-you-need-a-virtual-assistant/">signs your business needs a dedicated remote team</a> first.</p>

        <h2>Step 2: Choose your hiring channel</h2>
        <p>There are four realistic channels, and they differ mainly in who does the work and who carries the risk:</p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Channel</th><th>You handle</th><th>Typical timeline</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Freelance marketplaces</td><td>Everything: posting, screening, contracts, replacement</td><td>1 to 4 weeks, high variance</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Job boards in target countries</td><td>Everything, plus foreign payroll and compliance</td><td>4 to 8 weeks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>US-based hourly agency</td><td>Daily management; the agency employs</td><td>1 to 3 weeks</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dedicated staffing partner</td><td>Final interviews and the work itself; the partner recruits, employs, and guarantees</td><td>About 5 weeks to onboarded</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>The right channel depends on how much recruiting capacity you have in-house. Businesses without a remote recruiting track record usually get faster, safer results from a partner, an honest trade-off we quantify in the <a href="../../blog/remote-team-vs-in-house-cost/">remote vs in-house cost comparison</a>.</p>

        <h2>Step 3: Screen for the skills remote work actually needs</h2>
        <p>Beyond role skills, four traits predict remote success better than any credential:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Written communication.</strong> Remote work runs on writing. Test it with a written exercise, not an interview question about it.</li>
          <li><strong>Self-management.</strong> Ask for examples of work they structured themselves, with no one watching.</li>
          <li><strong>Proactive problem-solving.</strong> Give a scenario with a missing piece of information and see whether they flag it or stall.</li>
          <li><strong>Reliability.</strong> Small commitments during the process, showing up on time, sending things when promised, are the best predictor of big ones later.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Step 4: Run a paid work test</h2>
        <p>Never hire a remote employee on interviews alone. A paid trial of 5 to 10 business days, or a substantial paid test project, tells you more than five interviews: work quality, communication rhythm, and how they handle feedback. Define pass and fail criteria before the trial starts, and pay fairly for the work. It is the cheapest insurance in the entire process.</p>

        <h2>Step 5: Interview on video, and interview for fit deliberately</h2>
        <p>Keep interviews focused: verify the test work (ask them to walk you through their choices), probe the four traits above, and align on working hours, communication norms, and escalation paths. Two interviews are enough when the work test is real. This is the step our clients keep for themselves even when we run everything else: our <a href="../../services/staffing-recruitment/">staffing and recruitment</a> process ends with you interviewing finalists and choosing who joins your team.</p>

        <h2>Step 6: Handle employment and compliance properly</h2>
        <p>Hiring across borders means contracts, payroll taxes, and labor law in the employee's country. Your three options are a local entity (rarely worth it for a small team), an employer of record service, or a staffing partner who employs the professional directly. Whichever route you choose, get it in writing before the start date. Shortcuts here become expensive later.</p>

        <h2>Step 7: Onboard like it matters, because it decides everything</h2>
        <p>The first 30 days decide whether a good hire becomes a good employee:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Week 1:</strong> access, tools, and shadowing. One buddy or manager as the single point of contact.</li>
          <li><strong>Week 2:</strong> first owned tasks with documented processes and examples of good output.</li>
          <li><strong>Weeks 3 to 4:</strong> full workload, a daily async update rhythm, and one weekly video check-in.</li>
          <li><strong>Day 30:</strong> a structured review against the success measures from Step 1.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Founders who spend 5 to 10 hours on knowledge transfer in the first two weeks get dramatically better output from the same hire. Offshore and remote hiring amplify the quality of your onboarding; they do not substitute for it. For model and geography choices behind these steps, see <a href="../../blog/nearshore-vs-offshore-vs-onshore/">nearshore vs offshore vs onshore</a>.</p>

        <h2>What this process costs when a partner runs it</h2>
        <p>At Remote Team Partners the process above is the service: role design, sourcing, screening, and testing are ours, the final interview is yours, and employment and payroll stay with us. The result is one monthly rate per professional, $1,500 for Associates, $2,500 for Professionals, and from $5,000 for Experts, with a replacement guarantee if a hire does not work out. Full pricing context is in the <a href="../../blog/virtual-assistant-cost/">virtual assistant cost guide</a>.</p>
