  <section class="sec" id="faq" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> FAQ</span>
        <h2>Hiring remote employees, answered.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How long does it take to hire a remote employee? <span class="plus">+</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-a">Doing it yourself typically takes 4 to 8 weeks from job post to start date. Through a dedicated staffing partner, the timeline is about 5 weeks from the first call to a fully onboarded professional, because sourcing and screening pipelines already exist.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Should I hire directly or use a staffing agency? <span class="plus">+</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-a">Hire directly when you have remote recruiting experience, time for a 4 to 8 week search, and a way to handle foreign payroll and compliance. Use a partner when you need to hire within weeks, lack that infrastructure, or want a replacement guarantee.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How long should a trial period be? <span class="plus">+</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-a">A paid trial of 5 to 10 business days gives enough signal on quality, communication, and fit. One or two days is too short to reveal patterns, and three to four weeks delays the decision without proportionally better information.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the biggest mistake companies make hiring remotely? <span class="plus">+</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-a">Skipping the role specification. A vague job description attracts mismatched candidates, and every later step inherits the problem. A written role spec with deliverables, tools, and 30/60/90 day measures prevents most failed hires.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Who is the legal employer of a remote hire through a partner? <span class="plus">+</span></summary>
          <div class="faq-a">The staffing partner is. Remote Team Partners employs the professional and handles contracts, payroll, benefits, and compliance, while the professional works exclusively for your business under your direction.</div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

